<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rhind Mathematical Papyrus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1550 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century-bce">16th century BCE</a> · Also known as Ahmes Papyrus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Rhind Mathematical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">Papyrus</a> is the largest surviving mathematical text from ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, copied by the scribe Ahmose in the 33rd year of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyksos">Hyksos</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apepi">Apepi</a> from an original of the Twelfth Dynasty. It contains over eighty worked problems on fractions, arithmetic, areas, volumes and the division of goods. Bought at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxor">Luxor</a> in 1858 by the Scottish antiquarian Alexander Henry Rhind, it is now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rhind-mathematical-papyrus.jpg" alt="Rhind Mathematical Papyrus" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: unknown (c. 2000 B.C) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rhind_Mathematical_Papyrus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
